%S A398789 1,1,9,241,23289,9471241,16823549049,128498815890481,
%T A398789 4153078425352726809
%N A398789 Number of serial binary relations on an n-set having the diamond property.
%C A398789 A binary relation R has the diamond property if xRy and xRz imply that there exists w such that yRw and zRw. Serial means that for every x there exists y such that xRy.
%C A398789 For a relation R, let S_x = {y : xRy}, and define the intersection graph G_R on [n] by {x,y} in E(G_R) iff S_x intersect S_y is nonempty, for distinct x,y. The diamond property is equivalent to each S_x being a clique of G_R.
%C A398789 The diamond property is also called confluence or weak directedness.
%F A398789 a(n) = Sum_G n!/|Aut(G)| * N(G), where the sum is over isomorphism classes of simple graphs G on n vertices, and N(G) is the number of ordered n-tuples (S_1,...,S_n) of nonempty cliques of G such that S_i intersect S_j is nonempty iff {i,j} is an edge of G.
%e A398789 For n = 2, the 9 relations counted by a(2) are represented by the ordered pairs (S_1,S_2) = ({1},{1}), ({1},{2}), ({1},{1,2}), ({2},{1}), ({2},{2}), ({2},{1,2}), ({1,2},{1}), ({1,2},{2}), ({1,2},{1,2}), where S_x = {y : xRy}.
%Y A398789 Cf. A399617 (ordered n-tuples of nonempty subsets of an n-set with pairwise nonempty intersection); this is the contribution corresponding to G = K_n.
%Y A398789 Cf. A052880 (transitive reflexive early-confluent relations).
%K A398789 nonn,hard,more,new
